{
  "dom_id": "step-q324352",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The scores on a standardized exam are normally distributed with an unknown mean $\\mu $ points and a standard deviation of $12$ points. It's observed that $25%$ of the students score less than $68$ points. Find the mean $\\mu ,$ **rounding your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.05$ | $0.10$ | $0.15$ | $0.20$ | $0.25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$points$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the exam score. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{12}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<68)=0.25.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<68) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{68-\\mu }{12}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{68-\\mu }{12}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{68-\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{68-\\mu }{12} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 68-\\mu  & =-8.094 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈76.09\\,points.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
